window.docContents[224].resolve({"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations","header":"3.4. Distributions and Fourier Analysis","id":"/Foundations/Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Distributions-and-Fourier-Analysis"},"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--What-is-formalized___-and-in-what-order":{"contents":"Each solution concept comes after the analysis it needs. The equations come\nfirst: particle paths and the material derivative, the continuity equation and\nits constant-density reduction, the Cauchy stress and the strain-trace\nidentities that turn Cauchy's momentum law into the Navier–Stokes form, and the\ncommon weak-form test data. Then the functional analysis of divergence-free\nfields: the solenoidal kernel, its weak and Bochner closure, the cubic\ncancellation of the convection form on that kernel, and abstract pressure\nrecovery from a closed-range divergence. Inviscid flow follows, from Euler\nresiduals and solution concepts through\npressure as an elliptic constraint,\nvorticity transport and stretching, Bernoulli's law, time-dependent potential\nflow with recovered pressure and initial trace, and exact rotation. The\nChen–Hou Boussinesq formulation contributes its rescaling and normalization\nidentities, a fixed-power similarity reduction, and Poisson inversion by the\nhalf-plane Green potential for compactly supported  sources with an odd\nextension across the boundary. Viscous flow brings back the Stokes and\nNavier–Stokes residuals and their hierarchy, a certified shear flow, classical\nsolutions with pressure recovery, the vorticity form, and the weak and\nLeray–Hopf classes with the energy\ninequality, to which the zero flow belongs. The Taylor–Green vortex and\nKovasznay's wake are certified exact Navier–Stokes flows. The steady Stokes\nproblem is solved by minimizing its energy on bounded domains, and the pressure\nof a minimum is recovered in  on arbitrary open domains.\nFor axisymmetric flow with swirl, profiles in the squared radius give a\ndivergence-free Cartesian velocity, of finite energy under weighted moment\nbounds; when the profiles satisfy the reduced equations, the velocity satisfies\nthe three-dimensional vorticity equation and has a pressure on convex sets.\nLast comes the three-dimensional regularity problem: Fefferman's\nalternatives (A) and (C) on  as two Lean propositions over the\nclassical solution class, an abstract continuation alternative for norm\ntrajectories, and the zero datum as a solved instance of the existence\npredicate.\n\nThe De Gregorio model of transport and stretching in one dimension has its\nequation-level theory here. Its blowup on the real line, the  conclusion\nof Chen–Hou–Huang's Theorem 1, is formalized on top of that theory in the\ncompanion repository BlowupZoo: weighted stability and continuation construct a\nglobal normalized trajectory from smooth initial data near the reference\nprofile, and the physical reconstruction gives finite-time blowup and\nconvergence to a nonzero stationary profile in dynamic coordinates.\n\nThe route here draws on : transport from §2.1,\nnonlinear conservation laws from\nChapter 3, potential functions from §4.4.2, diffusion from §2.3 and Chapter 7,\nelliptic pressure recovery from Chapter 6, the Sobolev and compactness tools\nfrom Chapter 5, and the variational Stokes problem from §8.4.4.\n supplies the fluid-specific kinematics\nand vorticity viewpoint;  and Fefferman supply the\nweak-solution and global-regularity statements, \nthe solenoidal function spaces, and  the nearly\nself-similar Boussinesq analysis.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Incompressible Fluid Mechanics\u0009From conservation laws to incompressible flow","header":"8.1.1. What is formalized, and in what order","id":"/Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics/From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow/#PDEAtlas--Incompressible-Fluid-Mechanics--From-conservation-laws-to-incompressible-flow--What-is-formalized___-and-in-what-order"},"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Parabolic Equations\u0009Finite-Time Behavior\u0009Finite-Time Growth, Lifespan, and Similarity","header":"6.7.1.5. Finite-Time Singularity Interfaces","id":"/Parabolic-Equations/Finite-Time-Behavior/#PDEAtlas--Parabolic-Equations--Finite-Time-Behavior--Finite-Time-Growth___-Lifespan___-and-Similarity--Finite-Time-Singularity-Interfaces"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Abstract-Extension-Interfaces":{"contents":"The two basic extension interfaces live at the same two levels as the\nrepresentative and graph-space Sobolev models. Each contains only the data\nthat later theorems use.\n\nFor natural order , exponent , and domain\n, the representative-level extension property is\nthe existence of a mapsuch thatfor every representative-level Sobolev function .Only the existence of  and the almost-everywhere\nagreement of base functions are required; the map need not be linear or\nbounded, and its values outside  are unconstrained.The chosen global object is a global Sobolev representative. When \nis open, its weak derivatives restrict to , and almost-everywhere\nagreement of the base functions and uniqueness of weak derivatives identify\nthem with the derivatives of . This is the information used by the\nextension-and-restriction density theorems.\n\nThe graph-space interface asks for the same almost-everywhere agreement on\n, realized by a continuous linear map between the completed spaces,\nwhich is the form in which norm estimates and compactness travel from\n to .\n\nA graph-space extension operator of order  is a continuous linear mapwhose base coordinate agrees almost everywhere with the base coordinate of the\ninput on . The structure stores:* toContinuousLinearMap, the bounded linear map between the completed graph\nspaces;* agree_ae, the almost-everywhere agreement statement for the base\ncoordinate with respect to Lebesgue measure restricted to .The proposition HasExtensionOperator is Nonempty applied to this structure:\nit asserts existence without selecting a canonical operator in theorem\nstatements.Agreement of the derivative coordinates is not stored separately. Both the\noutput and input graph bundles certify that their stored coordinates are weak\nderivatives of their base coordinates, so restriction of the global\nweak-derivative identities, followed by uniqueness on the open domain,\nrecovers derivative-coordinate agreement whenever it is required.\n\nNeither interface constrains the extension outside . Support of the\nextension in a prescribed neighborhood of  is recorded as\nseparate data because it comes from\na separate step, multiplication by a cutoff, which is available at every\norder.\n\nPrescribed support is data added to an extension operator. A supported\noperator over a set  consists of an extension\noperator together withfor the base coordinate of every input . This indicator identity is\nindependent of modifications on null sets and is equivalent to saying that the\nchosen base representative vanishes almost everywhere outside .At every Sobolev order , including  and , any bounded\nextension operator can be localized when\n is measurable,  is compact, and\n is an open neighborhood of . Choose a smooth compactly\nsupported cutoff  in  with  on\n, and defineBoundedness follows from boundedness of multiplication by  on\n, agreement is preserved because the cutoff equals one\non , and the cutoff support gives the indicator identity\n(Operator.withSupportIn).\n\nOne implication between the interfaces is needed later. Almost-everywhere\nagreement of base coordinates is all that survives the passage, and it is\nall the density argument uses.\n\nEvery bounded graph-space extension operator induces a representative-level\nextension.Given  in the representative model, first store its weak derivatives in\ngraph space by ofRepresentative, apply the bounded graph-space operator, and then forget\nthe global derivative coordinates with toRepresentative. The operator's\nbase-coordinate agreement and the model bridge show that the resulting global\nrepresentative equals  almost everywhere on .The converse would need linearity and norm control, which a\nrepresentative-level extension does not carry.\n\nSend the input representative to the domain graph space. Apply the\ncontinuous linear extension and convert the result back to a global\nrepresentative. Combine the extension operator's agree_ae field with the\nalmost-everywhere base-coordinate identity for ofRepresentative.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Extension, Trace, and Embeddings\u0009Extension and Density on Regular Domains","header":"4.3.1.1. Abstract Extension Interfaces","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Extension___-Trace___-and-Embeddings--Extension-and-Density-on-Regular-Domains--Abstract-Extension-Interfaces"},"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Supported-closure-inside-the-whole-space-scale":{"contents":"For every real , letThis is a complex closed subspace of the whole-space Fourier model.  Its\nelements remain whole-space tempered distributions; it is not a raw function\nspace on .\n\nThe supported closure is a closed, hence complete, subspace of .\nIt is monotone in the support set:\n\nClosedness is built into Submodule.topologicalClosure.  Completeness follows\nfrom being a closed subspace of the Hilbert space .  Domain monotonicity\nholds first for every supported Schwartz generator, then for its complex span,\nand finally for the topological closure.\n\nClosedness is what gives access to the Hilbert-space operations: the inclusion\nalong  is an isometry because it does not change\nthe underlying whole-space element, and completeness supplies the orthogonal\nprojection from  onto the closure.\n\nThe supported closure carries its canonical Hilbert-subspace operations.\nEvery element is an -norm limit of the complex span of supported\nSchwartz functions.  If , the induced mapis isometric, fixes the underlying whole-space element, and composes\nfunctorially along chains of support-set inclusions.The orthogonal projectionis contractive, fixes the supported closure, and has orthogonal residual.\nIt is the best approximation in the ambient  norm.  For nested support\nsets, projecting first onto the larger closure does not change the subsequent\nprojection onto the smaller one.\n\nThe approximation statement is the metric characterization of membership in\nthe topological closure of the supported Schwartz span.  Support-set inclusion\nacts by codomain restriction of the canonical submodule embedding, so it\npreserves norms definitionally and its identity and composition laws follow\nby subtype extensionality.  Completeness of the closed subspace supplies\nMathlib's orthogonal projection.  Its standard projection theorem gives the\ncontraction, fixed-point, orthogonal-residual, and best-approximation\nproperties; monotonicity of the closed submodules gives the nested-projection\nlaw.\n\nThe supported closure is nonzero as soon as  has an interior point.\n\nFor every real , distinct Schwartz functions define distinct elements of\n. If  has nonempty interior, then\n contains a nonzero element, so it is\nnot the zero subspace. For  it is the zero subspace.\n\nThe maps from Schwartz functions to  and from  to tempered\ndistributions are injective. A smooth bump centred at an interior point of\n, supported in a ball contained in , is a nonzero Schwartz\nfunction with support in . For the empty set the only such Schwartz\nfunction is zero, and the zero subspace is closed.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Sobolev Spaces and Weak Derivatives\u0009Fourier Sobolev Spaces\u0009Fourier and Fractional Sobolev Spaces","header":"4.5.1.11. Supported closure inside the whole-space scale","id":"/Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives/Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces/#PDEAtlas--Sobolev-Spaces-and-Weak-Derivatives--Fourier-Sobolev-Spaces--Fourier-and-Fractional-Sobolev-Spaces--Supported-closure-inside-the-whole-space-scale"}});